    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 63 tests pass, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the **level-ceiling** rule (Cp51): a new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at one above the current maximum among their existing household members.\n\n### Core change \u2014 the cap\n- **`Owner`** (`model/Owner.java`): added a persisted `membershipLevelCap` field (`membership_level_cap` column) so the cap survives to read-time, where `membershipLevel` is derived.\n- **`MembershipLevel.of(...)`**: after computing the points-based level, it now clamps to `membershipLevelCap` when one is set (null cap = no clamp).\n- **`AssignMembershipCap`** (new pipeline function) + wiring in `owners.POST.yml`: after the household id is finalized and before save, it computes `max(level of existing non-deleted household members) + 1` and stamps it. No existing members \u2192 no cap.\n- Added the `membership_level_cap` column to the hsqldb/h2/postgres/mysql schemas and to the positional seed inserts in `hsqldb/data.sql` (the profile the tests use).\n\n### Two behaviors the test suite forced (the spec was silent on them, but the tests jointly pin them)\n1. **Cp23/Cp39** create declared `sharesHousehold` members that legitimately reach level 3, so the cap must **not** apply to declared members \u2014 `AssignMembershipCap` exempts `sharesHousehold: true`. Cp51's undeclared joiner is still capped.\n2. **Cp51's** second owner shares a household but carries its **own email**, and must be *created* (not 409'd), whereas **Cp10/Cp35's** emailless same-household owners must still 409. So `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` now treats an owner that brings its own email as a genuinely distinct household member and lets it join (the identity check still rejects a true telephone/email/household duplicate).\n\nVerified: the full suite is green (63/63), including the previously-passing household, identity, and membership-tier checkpoints.",
